<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The privacy manifest for the AbridgeNotes.xcframework the partner embeds. Copied
  to the root of each slice's framework bundle, which is where Apple reads it.

  Every declaration below is derived from a call site in the shipped binary, which
  is this framework's own sources plus AbridgeRecorderCore and Sentry: both pods
  are statically merged in (Podfile, deliberately no `use_frameworks!`), so Apple's
  scan of this binary sees their symbols, and neither carries a manifest of its own
  into the framework. Core's podspec ships `Sources/**/*.swift` with no resources;
  Sentry's resource bundle is not copied (see the Podfile's post_integrate), because
  it declares data that SentrySDK.start collects and this framework never calls it.
  So these declarations are this file's to make.
  AbridgeRecorderCore's vendored OpusEncoder is merged in too and reaches no
  required-reason API — `nm -u` over both libOpusEncoder.a slices finds no
  stat/fstat/getattrlist/statfs symbol.

  Both categories are declared because the symbol is in the binary, which is
  what Apple's submission check scans — not because a runtime path is known to
  reach it. The call site that earns each, and the reason chosen:

    NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp / C617.1
      AbridgeRecorderCore EncounterRecordingRepository.loadAll reads the
      creation date of each persisted recording to sort them oldest-first,
      through both keys Apple lists for this category —
      URLResourceKey.creationDateKey, passed to contentsOfDirectory, and
      FileAttributeKey.creationDate, read out of attributesOfItem. (The
      FileManager methods themselves are not on Apple's list; the keys are.)
      This runs on every configure, through the SDK's restoration path, so it is
      live usage rather than a declaration made for a symbol nothing reaches.
      C617.1 is "metadata of files inside the app container", which these are:
      the audio recordings AbridgeRecorderCore persists under the host's
      Documents directory. (Not the SDK's own encounter records, which live
      under Application Support and are read without any timestamp key.)
      Sentry's SentryCrashFileUtils also calls stat (a directory check before
      deleting a path's contents) and fstat (a file's size before reading it),
      on files under Sentry's own cache directory in the same container.

    NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategorySystemBootTime / 35F9.1
      AbridgeRecorderCore EncounterRecordingManager.emitAudioLevel reads
      ProcessInfo.systemUptime as the clock its 100ms audio-level throttle
      measures against — 35F9.1's "time that has elapsed between events that
      occurred within the app". 35F9.1 also forbids sending the value
      off-device, which holds absolutely here: emitAudioLevel returns at its
      `guard enableAudioLevelMetering` before the read, because the SDK is the
      one caller that builds the manager with metering off, and no SDK request
      body or delegate callback carries an audio level or a timestamp either way.
      8FFB.1 would be the reason for stamping absolute event times; that is not
      what this does.

  The other three categories are absent because none of their symbols is in the
  built binary: no UserDefaults, no disk-space (volumeAvailableCapacity / statfs)
  and no active-keyboard (UITextInputMode.activeInputModes) reference. Sentry's
  one UserDefaults read, in SentryUncaughtNSExceptions, is compiled for macOS
  only.

  NSPrivacyTracking is false and NSPrivacyTrackingDomains empty because nothing
  in the framework tracks, in Apple's sense of linking data with third-party data
  for advertising or sharing it with a data broker. Sentry is bundled for the
  SDK's own error reports, sent to Abridge's Sentry project through a client the
  SDK owns; it is never started process-wide, so it installs no crash, signal or
  uncaught-exception handler. Its client still registers dyld image callbacks and
  reads the host app's state and view-controller names while preparing a report;
  the report is rebuilt from an allowlist that carries neither. The delivery
  build asserts that no analytics, attribution or other crash-reporting library
  is bundled. The framework's egress is the Abridge API host for the selected
  ServerEnvironment (api-dev / api-test / api.abridge.care) and Sentry's ingest
  host.

  NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes declares three types:

    AudioData, linked, app functionality
      The encounter audio, uploaded to Abridge to produce the note.

    UserID, linked, app functionality
      The provider id, patient id and customerReferenceId the host supplies
      with each encounter.

    OtherDiagnosticData, not linked, other purposes
      The SDK's own error reports, through the bundled Sentry client. This
      entry belongs to that error reporting and goes with it. Not CrashData or
      PerformanceData: no crash handler is installed and nothing is traced.

  None is used for tracking. The patient's date of birth and gender, optional
  on create, are not declared as a type of their own; the integration guide
  asks the host to cover them in its own disclosure. Section 4 of the
  integration guide, under "What the SDK sends off-device" and "Error
  reporting", lists every value the SDK transmits and on which request.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>C617.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategorySystemBootTime</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>35F9.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeAudioData</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeUserID</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeOtherDiagnosticData</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeOther</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
</dict>
</plist>
